            { label: 'If the accounting equation balances on the balance sheet, the books are correct by definition, so there is nothing wrong', consequence: 'The equation can balance and still lie if a liability is simply hidden. That is exactly what Enron did with its Special Purpose Entities, moving $38 billion of debt off the balance sheet so the company looked far less leveraged. Add the hidden loans back and the real picture is very different.', score: 0 },
            { label: 'The equation only balances because real liabilities are hidden. Add those loans back and the company is far more indebted than it looks, this is the Enron off-balance-sheet scheme. Reconstruct the true balance sheet', consequence: 'Exactly right. The accounting equation only tells the truth when it reflects ALL assets and liabilities. When debt is parked in hidden shell entities, the equation balances on paper while concealing the real risk, the precise fraud that destroyed Enron. Rebuilding the true balance sheet exposes it.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'The hidden loans are worth a footnote, but since the equation still balances you keep them as a minor observation', consequence: 'Hidden liabilities are the heart of the fraud, not a footnote. Enron\'s entire deception rested on keeping $38 billion in debt off the balance sheet while the equation appeared to balance. Treating this as minor would miss the central scheme.', score: 1 }
