            { label: 'Reject, concentrating 70% of assets in long-duration bonds funded by short-term deposits creates the exact asset-liability mismatch that killed SVB. Recommend a laddered portfolio with maximum 30% in bonds beyond 5 years', consequence: 'Excellent risk management. You\'ve identified the three SVB risk factors: (1) excessive concentration, (2) duration mismatch, and (3) unstable funding source. A laddered portfolio (bonds maturing at regular intervals) reduces interest rate risk, and the 30% cap ensures a rate shock won\'t wipe out equity. This is how Jamie Dimon manages JPMorgan\'s portfolio.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Invest in bonds but classify them as held-to-maturity so any losses won\'t show on the balance sheet', consequence: 'This is literally what SVB did, and it failed catastrophically. HTM classification doesn\'t eliminate losses; it just hides them from the income statement. Depositors, analysts, and regulators will eventually discover the unrealized losses in the footnotes. Hiding risk is not managing risk.', score: 0 }
