      'Rejecting the SVB-like concentration strategy, managing interest rate risk through hedging and duration limits, and addressing uninsured deposit concentration, scoring 9/9.',
      'Balance sheet risk management comes down to three principles: (1) Never concentrate assets in a single risk factor, diversify across durations, asset classes, and sectors. (2) Always stress-test equity against worst-case scenarios, if a single shock can wipe out more than 20% of equity, you\'re over-exposed. (3) Funding matters as much as assets, uninsured, concentrated deposits are unstable liabilities that can vanish overnight. SVB violated all three principles simultaneously, and the result was the fastest bank collapse in modern American history.'
