            { label: 'Raise the price by $7 to $86.99 to maintain the same contribution margin, but communicate the added value to customers to justify the increase', consequence: 'New CM = $86.99 - $42 = $44.99 (unchanged). BEP remains 44,454 units. If demand drops slightly to 75,000 units due to the price increase, profit = (75,000 x $44.99) - $2M = $1,374,250. You\'ve protected your margin structure while accepting slightly lower volume. This is the textbook approach: pass through cost increases while adding perceived value.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Switch to a cheaper supplier to keep variable costs at $35', consequence: 'Tempting but risky. Cheaper components may reduce quality, increasing warranty costs (hidden variable costs) and damaging brand reputation. However, this is worth exploring IF quality can be maintained. The best approach combines supplier negotiation with strategic pricing.', score: 2 }
