            { label: 'Only if projected demand exceeds the new break-even of ~102,000 units, the higher fixed costs require significantly more volume to break even, despite the better unit economics', consequence: 'Perfect analysis. You calculated the new break-even (101,712 units), recognized it exceeds current sales (75,000), and understood the operating leverage trade-off. This investment only makes sense if you have strong evidence demand will grow 35%+ within 1-2 years. The $14/unit savings is powerful but only if volume is sufficient.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'No, never increase fixed costs; keep the flexible cost structure', consequence: 'Too conservative. If demand is growing strongly, the factory investment could dramatically increase profitability. At 150,000 units: old model profit = (150K x $44.99) - $2M = $4.75M. New model profit = (150K x $58.99) - $6M = $2.85M. Wait, at 150K the old model is actually better! You need ~200K+ units for the factory to pay off. The answer is conditional, not absolute.', score: 2 }
