      { term: 'External Audit', definition: 'An independent examination of financial statements by a qualified CPA firm. The auditor expresses an opinion on whether the statements are fairly presented under GAAP or IFRS.', example: 'Ernst & Young audits Apple\'s annual financial statements, examining hundreds of billions in transactions to issue their opinion.', sentence: 'Our board\'s audit committee selected a new external auditor to ensure a fresh perspective after the previous firm had audited us for 15 years.' },
      { term: 'Internal Controls', definition: 'Systems, processes, and procedures designed to prevent and detect errors, fraud, and misuse of company assets. Key principle: segregation of duties.', example: 'No single employee should be able to create a vendor, approve an invoice, and issue payment. These duties must be separated among different people.', sentence: 'The SOX 404 assessment revealed a material weakness in our internal controls over cash disbursements, one employee had both authorization and payment access.' },
      { term: 'Unqualified (Clean) Opinion', definition: 'The best audit outcome. The auditor concludes that financial statements are fairly presented in all material respects according to GAAP.', example: 'Berkshire Hathaway has received unqualified opinions for decades, the product of transparent, conservative accounting practices.', sentence: 'Investors were relieved when we received an unqualified audit opinion, confirming that our financial statements could be trusted.' },
      { term: 'Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX)', definition: 'Landmark 2002 law passed after Enron/WorldCom. Created PCAOB, required CEO/CFO certification, mandated internal controls reporting (Sec 404), and established criminal penalties for accounting fraud.', example: 'SOX Section 302: CEOs face up to 20 years prison and $5M fines for knowingly certifying false financial statements.', sentence: 'Our CFO personally certifies every quarterly filing under SOX, she takes this responsibility extremely seriously because her freedom depends on its accuracy.' },
      { term: 'Materiality', definition: 'The amount above which a misstatement could reasonably influence an investor\'s decision. Auditors plan their work around materiality thresholds.', example: 'For a $10B company, materiality might be set at $50-100M. A $1M error wouldn\'t require correction; a $75M error would.', sentence: 'The auditor determined that the $25 million inventory discrepancy was above the materiality threshold and required a restatement of the financial statements.' },
      { term: 'PCAOB', definition: 'Public Company Accounting Oversight Board. Created by SOX to independently oversee all audit firms that audit public companies. Conducts inspections and can discipline firms.', example: 'PCAOB inspections have found audit deficiency rates of 20-40%, meaning auditors frequently fail to gather sufficient evidence even post-SOX.', sentence: 'After the PCAOB inspection identified three audit deficiencies in our engagement files, the firm implemented new quality control procedures.' }
