      [
        { date: '1999', event: 'Harry Markopolos first suspects Madoff\'s returns are impossible', context: 'Markopolos, a quantitative analyst at a rival firm, was asked by his boss to reverse-engineer Madoff\'s "split-strike conversion" strategy. Within 5 minutes, he realized the math didn\'t work, the options market was too small to execute the strategy at Madoff\'s scale, and the consistent returns with virtually no down months violated basic statistical probability.' },
        { date: 'May 2000', event: 'Markopolos submits first formal complaint to the SEC', context: 'The report detailed mathematical proof that Madoff\'s returns were impossible. He titled it: "The World\'s Largest Hedge Fund is a Fraud." The SEC took no action. Markopolos would submit increasingly detailed reports in 2001, 2005, 2007, and 2008.' },
        { date: '2005', event: 'Markopolos submits 21-page report listing 29 red flags', context: 'Red flags included: returns too consistent (statistically impossible), strategy too large for the options market, no independent custodian, the firm\'s auditor was a tiny 3-person operation (Friehling & Horowitz), and Madoff\'s own employees couldn\'t explain the trading strategy. The SEC briefly investigated and closed the case.' },
        { date: 'Dec 2008', event: 'Madoff confesses to $65 billion Ponzi scheme; arrested next day', context: 'The fraud collapsed not because regulators detected it, but because the 2008 financial crisis caused investors to request $7 billion in redemptions simultaneously. Madoff couldn\'t pay them, there was no money to pay. He confessed to his sons, who reported him to the FBI. He was sentenced to 150 years in prison. The SEC\'s Inspector General later published a devastating 477-page report documenting the agency\'s systematic failures to investigate Markopolos\'s warnings.' }
      ],
      'The Madoff case teaches that forensic accounting tools work, but only if someone acts on the findings. Markopolos used basic forensic techniques: statistical analysis (returns too consistent to be real), capacity analysis (the options market was too small), structural analysis (no independent custodian, tiny auditor), and behavioral analysis (Madoff refused to let investors visit his trading floor). Every one of these red flags was detectable with publicly available information. The lesson: trust the math, verify independently, and never assume a prestigious name guarantees legitimacy. Madoff was a former NASDAQ chairman. Reputation is not a substitute for due diligence.'
