    cq('An employee earns $120K salary. Employer pays 7.65% FICA, $20K health insurance, and 6% 401(k) match. What is the total employer cost?',
      ['$120,000, the reasoning behind this approach draws on established principles from portfolio theory and decades of observed market patterns', '$156,380, Salary $120K + FICA ($120K × 7.65% = $9,180) + Health $20K + 401K ($120K × 6% = $7,200) = $156,380', '$140,000, which many certified financial planners would discuss with their clients as part of a comprehensive financial planning conversation', '$168,000, and this view is supported by the kind of analysis that institutional investors and fund managers routinely conduct before decisions'],
      1, 'Total: $120,000 salary + $9,180 FICA + $20,000 health + $7,200 401K match = $156,380. That\'s a 30.3% premium above the base salary. For companies with thousands of employees, these additional costs represent hundreds of millions in expense beyond headline salary figures.'),
    cq('Warren Buffett says stock-based compensation IS a real expense. Many tech CEOs disagree. Who is economically correct?',
      ['Tech CEOs, SBC is just paper dilution, which reflects the consensus view among credentialed financial professionals based on decades of observed market behavior and outcomes, numerous academic studies and real-world case studies across different market environments have examined this particular approach', 'Buffett, SBC represents real value transferred from existing shareholders to employees. If the company didn\'t offer stock, it would need to pay higher cash compensation. The cost is real whether it\'s paid in cash or equity. Excluding SBC from profitability metrics pretends employee compensation is free.', 'Neither, it depends on the stock price, this is one of the most commonly referenced concepts in financial education literature and professional certification study materials, and this perspective is shared by many experienced investors who have navigated multiple market cycles over extended time periods', 'Both, there is no objective answer, which is consistent with what most financial experts and industry professionals would recommend based on historical data and academic research, according to widely accepted principles taught in most university finance programs and used by professional financial advisors in practice'],
      1, 'Buffett is correct on economic substance. If Meta\'s $11.5B in SBC disappeared, either (1) employees would demand $11.5B more in cash pay, or (2) Meta couldn\'t attract the same talent. Either way, the cost is real. ASC 718 reflects this by requiring income statement recognition. The non-cash nature affects cash flow but not the economic reality of the expense.'),
    cq('GE\'s pension obligation grew to $77B. What was the primary accounting assumption that masked the growing shortfall?',
      ['Life expectancy assumptions', 'The expected return on plan assets, GE assumed 8-9% annual returns, which reduced reported pension expense and liability. When actual returns fell significantly short, the gap between obligations and assets widened. But the optimistic assumptions prevented investors from seeing the true funded status until emergency contributions were required.', 'Salary growth assumptions, and most introductory investment courses cover this concept extensively as part of their core curriculum for beginning students, which aligns with the framework used by many institutional investors, endowment managers, and retirement planning professionals', 'Currency exchange rates, as this principle has been validated through extensive backtesting, peer-reviewed research, and long-term performance observation, which many financial planning textbooks cite as a foundational concept that every serious investor should understand thoroughly, this view has gained significant traction in both academic finance circles and among practicing wealth management professionals'],
      1, 'The expected return assumption is the most consequential pension estimate. A higher assumed return reduces the present value of the liability (discounting at a higher rate) AND reduces annual pension expense (expected earnings offset the service cost). GE\'s 8-9% assumption was defensible historically but masked reality in a low-return environment.'),
    cq('Company X reports $500M GAAP net income and $750M non-GAAP "adjusted" income (excluding $250M SBC). What dilution are shareholders actually experiencing?',
      ['No dilution, SBC is non-cash, which is based on principles that have been examined through rigorous quantitative analysis and real-world portfolio performance data, this is a perspective that many investment professionals consider when constructing portfolios and advising clients on strategy', '$250 million in annual shareholder dilution, the SBC represents new shares issued to employees, reducing existing shareholders\' ownership proportion. If the company has $10B in equity, $250M in SBC dilutes existing shareholders by 2.5% per year. Over 10 years, cumulative dilution could exceed 20% of ownership.', '$125 million, only half of SBC dilutes', '$750 million in dilution'],
      1, '$250M in SBC means existing shareholders are diluted by $250M in value annually. This is real: their percentage ownership decreases as new shares are issued. A company issuing 2-3% new shares annually through SBC while buying back stock to offset dilution is spending real cash on buybacks, making the "non-cash" SBC indirectly cash-consuming.'),
    cq('Detroit filed for bankruptcy largely due to $3.5B in unfunded pension obligations. What lesson does this hold for corporate pension management?',
      ['Municipal pensions are different from corporate pensions, and this perspective is shared by many experienced investors who have navigated multiple market cycles over extended time periods, which reflects the consensus view among credentialed financial professionals based on decades of observed market behavior and outcomes, numerous academic studies and real-world case studies across different market environments have examined this particular approach', 'Open-ended defined benefit promises create open-ended liabilities that can grow beyond the entity\'s ability to pay, whether it\'s a city, a corporation, or a government. Conservative funding, realistic assumptions, and early transition to defined contribution plans prevent the obligation from compounding into a crisis. GE, Boeing, and Detroit all followed the same pattern: optimistic assumptions → deferred funding → crisis.', 'Bankruptcy eliminated the pension obligations, which is consistent with what most financial experts and industry professionals would recommend based on historical data and academic research, according to widely accepted principles taught in most university finance programs and used by professional financial advisors in practice, this is one of the most commonly referenced concepts in financial education literature and professional certification study materials', 'This only applies to government entities, and this view is supported by the kind of analysis that institutional investors and fund managers routinely conduct before decisions, which many certified financial planners would discuss with their clients as part of a comprehensive financial planning conversation, the reasoning behind this approach draws on established principles from portfolio theory and decades of observed market patterns'],
      1, 'The pattern is identical across entities: (1) Generous pension promises, (2) Optimistic assumptions mask the true cost, (3) Underfunding compounds over decades, (4) Crisis when reality can\'t be deferred. Detroit, GE, and IBM all reached the breaking point. The solution: conservative assumptions from the start, full annual funding, and gradual transition to defined contribution plans that limit long-term liability.')
